        { "text": "$x=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt{37}}{2}$", "why": "Used $+b$ in the numerator instead of $-b$." },
        { "text": "$x=-3\\pm\\sqrt{37}$", "why": "Divided only the $-b$ term by $2a$, not the whole numerator." },
        { "text": "$x=\\frac{-3\\pm\\sqrt{37}}{4}$", "why": "Doubled the denominator, using $4$ instead of $2a=2$." }
      ],
      "solution_text": "$x^{2}+3x-7=0$\n$a=1$, $b=3$, $c=-7$\n$x=\\frac{-3\\pm\\sqrt{3^{2}-4(1)(-7)}}{2(1)}$\n$=\\frac{-3\\pm\\sqrt{37}}{2}$"
